<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Umma Khan V</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1761">1761</a>–<a href="/years/1801">1801</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Umma Khan the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma">Umma</a> Khan V, called the Great, was khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avar</a> in mountainous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a> from 1774 until his death in 1801, when the khanate was at the height of its power. He led raids deep into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and Shirvan and received tribute and payments from neighbouring rulers, including the kings of Kartli-Kakheti. He also dealt with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, which was then advancing into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/umma-khan-v.jpg" alt="Umma Khan V" width="320" height="380" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Halil-Bey Mussaijassul · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khalil-Bek_Musayasul._Umma_Khan_of_Avary_hosting_the_Georgian_prince_Alexander.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
